You are given an integer array coins representing coins of different denominations and an integer amount representing a
total amount of money. Return the number of combinations that make up that amount. If that amount of money cannot be
made up by any combination of the coins, return 0. You may assume that you have an infinite number of each kind of coin.
The final answer is guaranteed to fit into a signed 32-bit integer.

Example 1:
Input: amount = 5, coins = [1,2,5]
Output: 4
Explanation: there are four ways to make up the amount:
5=5
5=2+2+1
5=2+1+1+1
5=1+1+1+1+1

Example 2:
Input: amount = 3, coins = [2]
Output: 0
Explanation: the amount of 3 cannot be made up just with coins of 2.

Example 3:
Input: amount = 10, coins = [10]
Output: 1

Constraints:
1 <= coins.length <= 300
1 <= coins[i] <= 5000
All the values of coins are unique.
0 <= amount <= 5000

Approach:
This is a classic unbounded knapsack problem where we make two recursive calls of pick and notPick. The unbounded
nature is shown when picking an element where we don't decrease the elements after picking. The base case stops the
recursion when the target is reached where there will be one way and if before reaching the target n goes to 0 then
there is no way. In tabulation space optimisation we now see that there is a requirement of using the current dp row
when picking hence we need to keep the inner loop from lower to higher index. Also, as this is the direction of the loop
the condition on the pick i.e coins[i-1] <= j (capacity) gets absorbed in the loop condition by starting with the
coins[i-1] which will be the current capacity that we start with and the loop will end at the target capacity.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n + 2^targetAmount) = O(2^(n + targetAmount), SC = O(n + targetAmount) (recursion stack space)
Each call makes up to 2 recursive calls, and every recursive call can go n levels deep + targetAmount levels deep as when
we analyse the recursive tree for the pick call then n here is not decreasing instead the targetAmount decreases. If we
consider the lowest value of the coins for the targetAmount which is 1 then this recursive branch goes targetAmount
levels deep.
Memoization -> TC = O(n * targetAmount), SC = O(n * targetAmount) + O(n + targetAmount) (recursion stack space)
= O(n * targetAmount)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n * targetAmount), SC = O(n * targetAmount)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n * targetAmount), SC = O(2 * targetAmount) = O(targetAmount)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n * targetAmount), SC = O(targetAmount)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_3 -> TC = O(n * targetAmount), SC = O(targetAmount)

Note:
This problem finds all the combinations of the ways for forming the target from the given set of coins where the
direction is fixed meaning no coin is again considered once used which points to the nature of combination where order
does not matter. But if asked for all ways where order does matter which will be a permutation question then the
recursive call and the dp state will be single. Meaning only the target will change recursively with a loop over all
the coins for each new decremented target or capacity. In this way every coin will be considered for each target(capacity).
There will be no pick and notPick recursive calls but only a single call if able to consider a coin. The dp will be a
1D array and in tabulation the amount loop will be outside and the coins loop will be inside.